[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was merged as one issue and closed on 5 October. Read by tenant and by
what the reports say, not by subject line, it held more than one problem, and closing it did not
close all of them.

## What #4471 held

<ProblemTable
  ticket={4471}
  {repo}
  issues={{ parser: 1, hint: 2, 'one-day move': 3 }}
  status={{ parser: 'fixed 5 Oct', hint: "won't fix: form retired", 'one-day move': 'open' }}
/>

## The problems

- **`parser`: the shared parser flipped day and month** ([#1]({repo}/issues/1), fixed). It read
  every date month first, and our tenants write the day first. Booked 7 April, the invite said
  4 July. Every tenant, through the app, email and the booking-v2 form. Engineering fixed it on
  5 October: `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`.
- **`hint`: the booking-v3 form asked for the month first** ([#2]({repo}/issues/2), won't fix).
  It printed `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field; people typed what it said, and the form read the
  date the tenant's way, day first. The same flip, a different bug, which the parser fix did not
  touch. Engineering retired the booking-v3 form instead of fixing it, so no new reports come in
  this way.
- **`one-day move`: dates move by one day** ([#3]({repo}/issues/3), **open**). Not a flip. The
  same meeting shows on Tuesday in Lisbon and on Monday in Toronto; an invite arrives before the
  booking was made; "every booking in June is one day off". The desk read these as a one-day move
  and counts them where they are filed, under `parser`. Neither the parser fix nor retiring the
  form deals with them. Most likely it is in how invite times are converted between time zones.

## Why it was one ticket

Duplicates are merged on subject line ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)), and
subject lines don't tell these problems apart: "Date is wrong" and "Month and day swapped?" come in
under every one of them. The subject says the date is wrong; only the body and the tenant say why.
Intake tags are a browser guess; counts here are by tenant.

<Callout kind="warn">
#4471 is closed, but #3 is not fixed. A report of a date that moved by one day goes to #3, not to the parser fix.
</Callout>

